    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. one more thing—since the buyer is the one holding the risk during that transit time, what kind of things should we be looking out for in the insurance paperwork to make sure we aren't left empty handed if something goes wrong? like are there specific clauses or things in the fine print that usually cause issues during claims?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You should scrutinize the insurance paperwork for the level of coverage, ensuring it meets or exceeds the minimum required by CIF, which is usually ICC (C). Look closely at exclusions, as these are where the most trouble arises—they often exclude damage from improper packing, inherent vice of the goods, or certain types of perils. It's wise to check the precise wording regarding \"all risks\" versus named perils, as a narrow definition can leave you exposed even if the damage seems obvious. If you are concerned, you might even negotiate for the seller to purchase higher coverage, like ICC (A).",
      "source": "target",
